### Week-by-Week Topics for CS381  Here's a summary of the topics covered in CS381 for Spring 2026:
1. **Week 1 (Jan 13, 15)**: Introduction to **Searching and Sorting Algorithms** 2. **Week 2 (Jan
20, 22)**: Basics of **Induction and Recursion** 3. **Week 3 (Jan 27, 29)**: Exploring **Graph
Algorithms** (DFS, BFS) 4. **Week 4 (Feb 3, 5)**: **NP-Completeness** and related problems. 5.
**Week 5 (Feb 10, 12)**: **Network Flow** and optimization problems. 6. **Week 6 (Feb 17, 19)**:
**Randomized Algorithms** and data structures. 7. **Week 7 (Feb 24, 26)**: More on **Disjoint
Paths** and **Minimum Cuts**. 8. **Week 8 (Mar 3, 5)**: **Midterm Review** and examination. 9.
**Spring Break (Mar 10-16)**: No classes. 10. **Week 9 (Mar 24, 26)**: **Dynamic Programming**
techniques. 11. **Week 10 (Mar 31, Apr 2)**: Focus on **Greedy Algorithms**. 12. **Week 11 (Apr 7,
9)**: Introduction to **Approximation Algorithms**. 13. **Week 12 (Apr 14, 16)**: **Hashing and
Heavy Hitters**. 14. **Week 13 (Apr 21, 23)**: **Matchings** and their applications. 15. **Week 14
(Apr 28, 30)**: **TBD** (to be determined). 16. **Final Exam Dates**: TBD.  You missed class during
**Week 2** (on January 20, 2026). To catch up, review class materials provided through the course
website, and focus on topics of **Induction and Recursion**.   ---  ### Catching Up for CS348  For
**CS348 (Information Systems)**: - Ensure you understand database fundamentals. Review the syllabus
and any recorded lectures available. - Focus on reading assignments and notes related to SQL and
relational databases. - Participate in any available PSOs (Peer Study Opportunities) for additional
help.  ### Catching Up for CS441 and CS440  For **CS441 (Data Science Capstone)**: - Review project
requirements and expectations provided on the course syllabus. - Attend any office hours or seek
help from TAs for clarification on your specific project.  For **CS440 (Large-Scale Data
Analytics)**: - Read the sections on big data concepts, including Hadoop and Spark. - Check any
recordings or notes shared during classes, focusing on lecture topics important for upcoming
assignments.  ---  If you have any additional questions or need further assistance with specific
topics, feel free to ask!
