Purdue University Course Outline
Department of Computer Science

### CS 44100: Data Science Capstone
**Spring 2026**  
**CRN:** 20433  

**Course Prerequisites:** CS 373  
**Course Brightspace link:** [Purdue Brightspace](https://purdue.brightspace.com/d2l/home/1488420)  

**Instructor**  
**Professor:** Mary Anne Smart  
**E-Mail:** [masmart@purdue.edu](mailto:masmart@purdue.edu)  
**Office Hours:** Tuesday, 2:30pm – 3:30pm in DSAI 1119D or by appointment  

**Graduate Teaching Assistants (GTAs)**  
- **Sai Coumar**  
  **E-Mail:** [scoumar@purdue.edu](mailto:scoumar@purdue.edu)  
- **Shrutheesh Iyer**  
  **E-Mail:** [iyer270@purdue.edu](mailto:iyer270@purdue.edu)  
- **Pinaki Mohanty**  
  **E-Mail:** [pmohanty@purdue.edu](mailto:pmohanty@purdue.edu)  

### Lectures  
Lectures are held on Mondays and Wednesdays from 4:30pm to 5:45pm in Hicks Undergraduate Library G980D. Some lecture time will be available for project meetings and collaboration as the semester progresses. Audio from class meetings will be recorded via BoilerCast. Contact the instructor for access to recordings if necessary.

### Course Description  
This capstone course provides students with the opportunity to integrate their accumulated knowledge and skills to identify and solve realistic data science problems, focusing on practical applications. Projects may be sponsored by corporate partners or academic/non-academic research groups. Students will follow the six phases of the CRISP-DM (Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining) methodology for a team-based project, gaining experience in project planning, report writing, and presentations.

### Learning Objectives  
After successful completion of this course, students will be able to:  
- Identify the business objectives in a complex problem.  
- Develop appropriate data science goals and project plans.  
- Implement appropriate data pre-processing and analysis models.  
- Evaluate models and interpret results with respect to business objectives.  
- Present project outcomes through written reports and digital/oral presentations.  
(Note: "business" refers to the corporate partner or research group sponsoring the capstone projects.)

### Textbooks  
There is no official textbook for this class. The following materials may be useful:  
- **Principles of Data Mining** by David Hand, Heikki Mannila, Padhraic Smyth  
- **A Course in Machine Learning** by Hal Daumé III  
- **Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning** by Christopher M. Bishop  
- **Machine Learning** by Tom Mitchell  
- **Pattern Classification** by Richard O. Duda, Peter E. Hart, David G. Stork  

### Grade Calculation  
**Team Participation (80%)**  
- Business Understanding Report: 10%  
- Data Understanding Report: 10%  
- Data Preparation Report: 15%  
- Modeling Report: 25%  
- Evaluation Report: 20%  

**Individual Participation (20%)**  
- Presentation of Team Project: 15%  
- Participation: 5%  

Note: Presentation scores will reflect the best performance across the semester. Overall team participation scores will be subject to a peer evaluation multiplier. Individual participation can be earned by attending class and completing peer evaluations.

### Grade Divisions  
- A+ [99-100]  
- A [93-99)  
- A- [90-93)  
- B+ [86-90)  
- B [83-86)  
- B- [80-83)  
- C+ [76-80)  
- C [73-76)  
- C- [70-73)  
- D+ [66-70)  
- D [63-66)  
- D- [60-63)  

These thresholds may be adjusted slightly in favor of more generosity.

### Late Policy  
Late submissions incur a 5% penalty per 24-hour period and may be submitted up to three days late. Additional extensions may be granted at the discretion of the instructional staff if arranged in advance. Documentation is required for agreements regarding late submissions. Extensions exceeding one week are only granted for serious and documented emergencies, processed through the Dean of Students’ Office: [Purdue Absences](https://www.purdue.edu/advocacy/students/absences.html).

### Regrade Requests  
Requests for regrades must be made within seven days of receiving the grade, specifying what needs reassessment and why. Generic requests may be dismissed. No regrading will occur after this period.

### Channels of Communication  
Questions about lecture content and reports should be posted in Ed discussion. For serious or confidential topics, email the instructor at [masmart@purdue.edu](mailto:masmart@purdue.edu) to schedule an appointment. Announcements will be made via Brightspace, and students are expected to monitor both Brightspace and email for class-related information. Respectful online interaction is encouraged, in line with the department's netiquette policy: [Department Netiquette Policy](https://www.cs.purdue.edu/resources/instructional/netiquette.html).

### Academic Integrity  
Purdue University prohibits dishonesty in any university activity, including cheating and plagiarism. Review the departmental academic integrity policy here: [Academic Integrity Policy](https://www.cs.purdue.edu/resources/academic_integrity.html). It is encouraged to discuss and help each other with basic concepts, but submitted work must reflect individual efforts.

### Artificial Intelligence Policy  
The use of AI must be clearly disclosed in reports and presentations. Failure to disclose AI use constitutes academic dishonesty.

### Nondiscrimination Statement  
Purdue University is committed to valuing the inherent worth of every person and fostering respect and understanding. More information is available on the course Brightspace under University Policies.

### Accessibility  
Purdue University is committed to accessibility for all students. Students experiencing barriers due to a disability should contact the Disability Resource Center at 765-494-1247 or [drc@purdue.edu](mailto:drc@purdue.edu) for accommodations.

### Mental Health Statement  
- **Therapy Assistance Online (TAO)** is a free resource for all Purdue students: [TAO Resource](https://www.purdue.edu/caps/students/resources/self-help/digital/tao.php).  
- If you need support or information about personal issues, please reach out to the appropriate resources.  

### Contact Information
For additional details, please refer to your Brightspace course materials or contact relevant faculty members.
================WEBPAGE: 6290=================

**CS 381: Fundamental Algorithms Course Overview**

The CS 381 course, offered by the Computer Science department, focuses on the study of fundamental algorithms. This undergraduate course covers a range of topics, including algorithm design and analysis, and is similar to the Spring 2025 course available at [Fundamental Algorithms Course](https://fundamentalalgorithms.com/fas24). The course utilizes various resources, such as lecture notes, textbooks, and online materials, to provide students with a comprehensive understanding of algorithms.

###

**Key Resources and Textbooks**

The following resources are provided to supplement the course:

- **Lecture Notes**
  - Usually one chapter per lecture.

- **Recommended Textbooks**
  - Jeff Erickson's "Algorithms" (2019)
  - Jon M. Kleinberg and Éva Tardos' "Algorithm Design" (2006)
  - Sanjoy Dasgupta, Christos H. Papadimitriou, and Umesh V. Vazirani's "Algorithms" (2008)
  - Thomas H. Cormen, Charles E. Leiserson, Ronald L. Rivest, and Clifford Stein's "Introduction to Algorithms" (2009)

- **Online Resources**
  - Video lectures from MIT's 6.006 and 6.046 courses.

###

**Course Structure and Policies**

- **Components**
  - The course consists of lectures, homework assignments, and exams.

- **Homework Assignments**
  - Due weekly, with specified late policy.

- **Exams**
  - Midterms and a final, consisting of multiple-choice and word problems.

- **Grading**
  - Grades are curved with the following weight distribution:
    - Homework: 25%
    - Midterm 1: 20%
    - Midterm 2: 25%
    - Final: 30%

- **Collaboration Policy**
  - Collaboration is allowed; students are encouraged to interact with each other and instructors during office hours and through EdStem.

###

**Grading and Assessment**

- **Homework Submissions**
  - Collected online through Gradescope.
  - Word problems are graded, with the bottom one-fifth of scores dropped.
  - Late submissions accepted with a penalty of 35% of the total points.
  - Exemplary submissions may be selected for solutions, with 10% extra credit awarded.
  - Regrade requests must be initiated within one week of grades being returned.

###

**Course Syllabus, Policies, and Procedures**

Welcome to CS381, an undergraduate course on fundamental algorithms. Refer to the schedule for a tentative list of topics. The syllabus includes:

- **Class Information**
  - Lecture times and staff information are to be confirmed.

- **Expected Participation**
  - Students are encouraged to utilize class time, PSOs, and office hours for questions or concerns.

- **Lecture Recording**
  - Lectures may be recorded and uploaded online (e.g., Boilercast, YouTube) to supplement in-class content.

- **Textbooks**
  - No strict textbook required as lecture notes will be provided. Additional resources will be suggested.

- **Correspondence**
  - Course website: www.fundamentalalgorithms.com
  - Emails should be reserved for true emergencies.

- **EdStem**
  - Class collaboration and discussion platform available at [EdStem Link](https://edstem.org/us/join/fsaryR).

###

**Grading Information**

- **Weight Distribution**
  - Homework: 25%
  - Midterm 1: 20%
  - Midterm 2: 25%
  - Final: 30%
  
  Numerical scores are computed and graded on a curved scale.

###

**Exams and Homework Policies**

- **Exams**
  - Each exam will include multiple-choice questions and word problems, with extended time allocated.

- **Homework**
  - Due every week by Thursday at 11:59 PM.
  - Submissions should be typeset in LaTeX or equivalent formats.
  
- **Collaboration and Group Work**
  - Collaboration among students is encouraged, with up to three students per submission. All collaborators must be indicated.

- **Late Submission Policy**
  - Submissions can be accepted up to 4 days late with a 35% penalty on the total points.

- **Exemplary Solutions**
  - Selected submissions will be published as solutions, with anonymity options available.

- **Regrades and Resubmissions**
  - Regrade requests must be made within a week. Students can utilize released solutions for later submissions, citing them appropriately.

- **Typesetting**
  - Submissions must adhere to mathematical and scientific formatting standards, ideally using LaTeX.

###
