### Contact Information for Resources

For additional resources and support, please contact the Office of the Dean of Students:
- Phone: [765-494-1747](tel:7654941747)
- Hours of Operation: Monday to Friday, 8 AM – 5 PM

### Mental Health Services

Purdue University is dedicated to the mental health and well-being of its students. If you or someone you know is struggling with feelings of being overwhelmed, depressed, or in need of mental health support, please reach out to Counseling and Psychological Services (CAPS):
- Phone: [765-494-6995](tel:7654946995) (available during and after hours, including weekends and holidays)
- Location: Second floor of the Student Health Center (PUSH) during business hours.

### Basic Needs Security

Any student facing challenges with securing food or housing is encouraged to contact:
- Office of the Dean of Students: [odos@purdue.edu](mailto:odos@purdue.edu)
- Basic Needs Program: [basic-needs@purdue.edu](mailto:basic-needs@purdue.edu)

Student Support Services are available Monday to Friday, 8 AM – 5 PM.

### Emergency Preparation

In the event of a major campus emergency, course requirements, deadlines, and grading percentages may be subject to change due to a revised semester calendar or other uncontrollable circumstances. Relevant changes will be communicated through the course website or can be obtained by reaching out to instructors or TAs via email. It is essential to frequently check your Purdue email for updates.

### Tentative Course Schedule for CS 44100 - Data Science Capstone

**Introduction (2 weeks)**:  
- Discuss CRISP-DM and its phases while selecting and settling on projects.

**Business and Data Understanding (4 weeks)**:  
- Explore projects and identify necessary information to address arising questions. Teams will plan their time schedule for the upcoming weeks. Reports and presentations will be required.

**Data Preparation (3 weeks)**:  
- Format data for analysis purposes. Reports and presentations will be required.

**Modeling (4 weeks)**:  
- Experiment with different modeling techniques using the team's data. Reports and presentations will be required.

**Evaluation (Remainder of Semester)**:  
- Assess the effectiveness of the model, possible success, and learning outcomes. Reports and presentations will be required.

### Disclaimer

This syllabus is subject to change. Any changes will be announced on Brightspace.
================WEBPAGE: 3185=================

WEBPAGE: https://www.cs.purdue.edu/academic-programs/courses/canonical/cs251.html

_________________________________________________________________________________

CS 251 — Data Structures (Canonical Course)
Prerequisites:
CS 18200 — Foundations of Computer Science
CS 24000 — Programming in C
Course Topics — Algorithm Analysis
Running time analysis of algorithms and implementations
Review of proof methods and mathematics concepts
Mathematical induction and recursion
Experimental running time analysis
Benchmarking and counting instructions
Importance of constants
Asymptotic running time analysis
Big-O and Θ notations
Best, expected, worst-case running times
Comparing growth: log n vs constants, linear vs quadratic vs exponential
Course Topics — Core Data Structures
Abstract data types (ADTs) and common operations
One- and two-dimensional arrays, dynamic arrays
Linked lists
Stacks and queues
Comparison of stack/queue implementations
Course Topics — Trees
Tree definitions and ADTs
Pointer-based tree implementations
Tree traversals and explorations
Binary trees: properties, ADTs, traversals
Representing & manipulating arithmetic expressions using trees
Course Topics — Searching and Sorting
Binary search
Basic sorting: Bubble sort, Selection sort, Insertion sort
Efficient sorting: Mergesort, Quicksort
Non-comparison sorts: Bucket sort, Counting sort, Radix sort
Course Topics — Heaps
Heap definitions, properties, ADTs
Array implementation of heaps
Priority queue operations: insertion, extract-min/max
Building heaps: incremental vs bottom-up
Heapsort
Course Topics — Binary Search Trees
BST properties, performance, ADTs
Basic operations: search, insert, delete
Course Topics — Balanced Search Trees
Balanced search tree definitions, properties, performance
Red-black trees and 2-3 trees
Maintaining balance under insertions and deletions
Course Topics — Hashing
ADTs and hash-table implementations
Hash functions
Collision handling: open hashing, linear probing, double hashing
Effects of table size and load factor on performance
Course Topics — Specialized Data Structures
Union-Find data structures
Spatial data structures and spatial queries
Course Topics — Strings & Tries
Trie representations, ADTs, and operations
Ternary and suffix trie variations
Data compression and Huffman code generation
Substring search algorithms
Boyer-Moore
Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP)
Course Topics — Graphs
Graph definitions, terminology, properties, ADTs
Graph representations: edge list, adjacency list, adjacency matrix
Undirected graphs:
DFS, BFS
Connected components
Directed graphs:
DFS and BFS revisited
Topological sorting and applications
Strongly-connected components (optional)
Selected graph algorithms:
Shortest paths: Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford
Minimum spanning trees: Prim, Kruskal
