### CS 18200 – Foundations of Computer Science  
**Purdue University, Department of Computer Science – Spring 2026 Course Outline**  

**Sections & Times:**  
- LE1: TR 10:30‑11:45 am, WALC 1055  
- LE2: TR 03:00‑04:15 pm, WALC 1055  
- LE3: TR 04:30‑05:45 pm, WALC 1055  

**Prerequisites:**  
- CS 18000, MATH 16100  

**Instruction Team:**  
- Director: Justin D. Gillingham  
- Professors: Wojciech Szpankowski, Sarah Sellke  
- GTAs: Arun Narayanan Hariharan, Andrew Haas  

**Contact:**  
- Email: [CS-18200-COOR@lists.purdue.edu](mailto:CS-18200-COOR@lists.purdue.edu) (staff list & office hours available on Brightspace)  

**Communication Channels:**  
- Brightspace, Ed Discussion, email (coordinators only) – standard response time 2‑3 business days; accommodations via ODOS/DRC (no medical documents requested).  

### Course Description  
This course introduces foundational discrete–mathematics tools for computer science, covering topics such as:  
- Logic  
- Proof methods  
- Functions  
- Sets  
- Relations  
- Number theory  
- Algorithm analysis  
- Induction  
- Counting  
- Probability  
- Trees/graphs  
- Finite-state automata  
- Regular expressions  
- Context-free languages  
- Turing machines  
- Computability  

### Required Text  
- K. Rosen, *Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications* (7th/8th ed.) - The student site includes examples, self-assessment resources, and a proof-writing guide.  

### Learning Outcomes  
Students will be able to:  
- Apply discrete mathematics principles.  
- Analyze algorithm complexity (Big O, Θ, Ω).  
- Use graph theory and algorithms to solve real-world problems.  

### Technology Platforms  
- Brightspace, Gradescope, Ed Discussion.  

### Grading Scheme (Tentative)  
- Homework: 20%  
- Quizzes: 10%  
- Midterm: 35% (Scheduled for Mon, Mar 9, 8‑10 PM)  
- Final: 35% (University-scheduled date, likely Sat, May 9, 2026)  
- Note: The lowest homework and quiz scores will be dropped.  

### Grade Scale  
- A+: [99-100]  
- A: [93-99)  
- A-: [90-93)  
- B+: [86-90)  
- B: [83-86)  
- B-: [80-83)  
- C+: [76-80)  
- C: [73-76)  
- C-: [70-73)  
- D+: [66-70)  
- D: [63-66)  
- D-: [60-63)  

### Homework Policy  
- 10-11 assignments will be posted on Brightspace and must be submitted via Gradescope (handwritten only).  
- Late submissions will incur a 15% penalty per 24-hour delay, with no submissions accepted after one day.  

### Quiz Policy  
- Approximately 10 quizzes (both online and in-class), with a 1-hour completion window and one retake allowed (highest score counts).  
- Online quizzes available from Sunday 1 PM to Monday 1 PM.  

### PSO Sessions  
- Weekly Practice/Study/Observation sessions led by a graduate TA, with undergrad TAs assisting; attendance is expected.  
- Problems will be posted on Brightspace, but solutions will not be shared.  

### Exams  
- One evening midterm and one comprehensive final exam, both in person, closed-book/notes, and no electronic devices permitted.  

### Accommodation/FERPA  
- Extensions must be approved by ODOS/DRC; faculty do not request medical documentation.  
- FERPA regulations prevent responding to parent emails.  

### Attendance Policy  
Students are expected to attend all classes and in-person exams unless excused by university regulations (e.g., illness, bereavement, military service). Only course coordinators can provide excuses. Students should inform coordinators in advance of any anticipated conflicts (e.g., university-sponsored activities, religious observations).  

### Conduct and Courtesy  
Students must maintain a professional and respectful classroom environment, including:  
- No talking during lectures.  
- Silencing personal electronics.  
- Arriving on time and remaining for the duration of the class.  
- Treating others with respect.  

### Netiquette  
When using Ed Discussion for communication, abide by the following standards:  
- Be polite and courteous.  
- Avoid using all caps (equivalent to screaming).  
- No offensive language or disruptive behavior.  
- Use clear subject lines and avoid abbreviations or acronyms not understood by all.  
- Keep discussions professional and collegial, avoiding inappropriate material or solutions to homework and exam questions.  

### AI Policy  
Students may use AI as a learning tool for understanding concepts and generating exercises. However, AI usage for completing homework assignments or during quizzes is not permitted.  

### Academic Integrity  
The course expects adherence to academic honesty policies, which state:  
- Homework solutions must be original work, with proper credit given for any online material used.  
- Sharing or copying solutions is considered cheating.  
- All instances of academic dishonesty will be reported to the Dean of Students Office, with penalties including zeros for assignments or possible failure of the course.  

### Copyright Expectations  
All course materials are copyrighted and are the property of the instructors. Students may use materials for personal purposes but cannot distribute them publicly without consent.  

### Final Note  
All students are expected to understand and comply with the policies outlined above to maintain a supportive learning environment.
