**Purdue University – Department of Computer Science – CS 18200 Foundations of Computer Science (Spring 2026) – Course Outline**  

### Course Sections  
- LE1: Tuesdays and Thursdays 10:30-11:45 am, WALC 1055  
- LE2: Tuesdays and Thursdays 3:00-4:15 pm, WALC 1055  
- LE3: Tuesdays and Thursdays 4:30-5:45 pm, WALC 1055  

### Prerequisites  
- CS 18000  
- MATH 16100  

### Instructional Team  
- Director: Justin D. Gillingham  
- Instructors: Wojciech Szpankowski, Sarah Sellke  
- Head GTAs: Arun Narayanan Hariharan, Andrew Haas  
- Coordinators: [CS-18200-COOR@lists.purdue.edu](mailto:CS-18200-COOR@lists.purdue.edu)  

### Communication  
- Questions about lectures and homework should be directed to PSO TAs and available during office hours.  
- Utilize Ed Discussion for general inquiries.  
- Email for accommodations/extensions should be directed to ODOS.  
- Standard email response time: 2-3 business days.  
- No responses to emails from parents (FERPA compliance).  

### Course Description  
This course introduces discrete mathematics tools for computer science, covering:  
- Logic  
- Proof methods  
- Functions and sets  
- Number theory  
- Algorithms  
- Growth functions  
- Induction and recursion  
- Counting and probability  
- Trees and graphs  
- Finite-state automata  
- Regular expressions  
- Context-free languages  
- Turing machines  
- Computability  

### Learning Outcomes  
- Apply discrete math principles.  
- Analyze algorithm complexity (Big O, Θ, Ω).  
- Solve graph-theory problems.  

### Required Text  
- K. Rosen, *Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications* (7th/8th ed.) – student site resources included.  

### Technology Platforms  
- Brightspace  
- Gradescope  
- Ed Discussion  

### Grading Scheme  
- Homework: 20% (lowest dropped)  
- Quizzes: 10% (lowest dropped)  
- Midterm: 35%  
- Final Exam: 35% (date set by the university)  

### Homework Policy  
- 10-11 handwritten assignments submitted via Gradescope.  
- 15% penalty for each 24-hour late period, with no submissions accepted after one day.  

### Quiz Policy  
- Approximately 10 quizzes (online and in-class).  
- One retake allowed; highest score counts.  
- Online quizzes available Sunday 1 PM – Monday 1 PM, 1-hour completion time.  

### PSO Sessions  
- Weekly problem practice led by a graduate TA; mandatory attendance.  
- Materials posted on Brightspace (no solutions posted).  

### Exams  
- One evening midterm and one comprehensive final exam.  
- All exams are in-person, closed-book, closed-notes, with no electronic devices allowed.  

### Accommodation Procedure  
- Contact ODOS for accommodations; coordinators forward approved requests.  
- No medical documentation required from instructors.  

### Grading Scale  
Letter grade cutoffs:  
- A+: 99-100  
- A: 93-99  
- A-: 90-93  
- B+: 86-90  
- B: 83-86  
- B-: 80-83  
- C+: 76-80  
- C: 73-76  
- C-: 70-73  
- D+: 66-70  
- D: 63-66  
- D-: 60-63  

### Additional Course Expectations  
- Active participation in PSO sessions and timely completion of assignments is crucial for success. Logs into course websites daily for updates are recommended.  
- Homework is typically due Fridays at 11:00 PM.  
- Plan to submit 60 minutes before the deadline to avoid penalties.  

### Summary of Course Components  
- **Homework**: 10-11 assignments; late submissions incur penalties.  
- **Quizzes**: Approximately 10; format includes retakes.  
- **Exams**: Scheduled midterm and final exam; no electronic devices permitted.
